  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 120 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nWeak zonal flow aloft continues today, bringing benign conditions\nto the region with temperatures near normal. Zonal flow \ntransitions to increasing southwest flow heading into the first \nhalf of the week as the next incoming trough comes onshore to the \nWestern U.S. This next trough splits off into a cut off low down \nin CA/Great Basin region, which increases warm, southwest flow \naloft and moisture. As a result, temperatures teeter to near to \nabove normal through Thursday. The increased moisture also brings \nlow end chances for showers in Southwest MT, particularly near \nthe ID border Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nBetter forcing and moisture along this cutoff system arrives late\nThursday into Friday. At the same time, another shortwave trough \ndips down from Canada and into MT. These two systems interact and \neventually merge into each other Friday, creating a broad wave. \nThis helps bring better chances for precipitation across the \nregion. A cold frontal passage Friday also help lower temperatures\nto below normal for the weekend. Snow levels also lower behind \nthe front for light mountain snow. This trough also looks to bring\nbreezy winds to North-Central MT Friday. Ensembles hint behind \nthis system another incoming trough moving onshore at the \nbeginning of next week, though models greatly diverge in amplitude\nand timing. However, below normal temperatures with slight \nchances of precipitation may continue into the beginning of next \nweek.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThere remains a decent spread in moisture amounts with this \nsystem, which will depend on how these two waves evolve with each\nother. There are two forcings with this system currently, the \nincoming closed wave with the frontal passage and the trough from \nCanada into the Rocky Mountain Front and North-Central MT. Models\nhave a decent range in how much precipitation is along and behind\nthe front. Current probabilities gives a 40-70% chance for 0.5\" \nof moisture for the Southwest Valleys and an 80% chance for the \nGallatin Range. Probabilities drop farther north into North- \nCentral MT, with a 20-40% chance for 0.5\" of moisture. Snow levels\nfalling behind the front gives moderate confidence for some light\nmountain snow to develop, with some model runs getting levels \ndown towards 6,000ft Friday in Southwest MT. Depending how much \ncold air from the shortwave trough dipping down from Canada moves \ninto the region, snow levels may lower to lower elevations late \nFriday night into Saturday morning, bringing a rain/snow mix. -Wilson",
